{"causes": ["The established tradition of Greek geometry, particularly the study of conic sections by Apollonius of Perga, provided a foundational framework for Perseus's investigations.", "The intellectual environment of the Hellenistic period fostered advanced mathematical inquiry and encouraged the exploration of complex geometric problems.", "The existence of the torus as a fundamental geometric solid naturally prompted mathematicians to investigate its various planar intersections."], "effects": ["Perseus's work expanded the scope of classical geometry by introducing and systematically classifying a new family of curves, the spiric sections.", "His investigations were referenced and preserved by later commentators like Proclus, ensuring his contribution was not entirely lost to history.", "The study of complex curves by Perseus contributed to the long-term mathematical tradition that would eventually lead to algebraic geometry and calculus."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "perseus", "sourceName": "Perseus", "verb": "CREATES", "targetSlug": "spiric-sections", "targetName": "Spiric Sections", "context": "Perseus is credited with inventing and systematically studying the geometric concept of spiric sections around 149-109 BCE."}, {"sourceSlug": "proclus", "sourceName": "Proclus", "verb": "CANONIZES", "targetSlug": "perseus", "targetName": "Perseus", "context": "Proclus, in his commentary on Euclid's *Elements* around 450 CE, explicitly credited Perseus with the invention of spiric sections, preserving his legacy."}, {"sourceSlug": "perseus", "sourceName": "Perseus", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "ancient-greece", "targetName": "Ancient Greece", "context": "Perseus was an ancient Greek geometer whose work on spiric sections occurred within the broader cultural and intellectual context of Ancient Greece around 149-109 BCE."}, {"sourceSlug": "apollonius-of-perga", "sourceName": "Apollonius of Perga", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "perseus", "targetName": "Perseus", "context": "Apollonius of Perga's comprehensive study of conic sections likely served as a direct inspiration and methodological model for Perseus's analogous work on spiric sections."}, {"sourceSlug": "perseus", "sourceName": "Perseus", "verb": "TRANSMITS", "targetSlug": "hellenistic-mathematics", "targetName": "Hellenistic Mathematics", "context": "Perseus's advanced geometric investigations contributed to and extended the body of knowledge within Hellenistic mathematics during the 2nd century BCE."}], "places": [{"name": "Alexandria, Egypt", "role": "Potential intellectual center"}, {"name": "Athens, Greece", "role": "Potential center of study"}, {"name": "Hellenistic World", "role": "Geographical scope of activity"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Perseus", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "perseus"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q3900144", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Perseus_(geometer)"], "tags": ["Logicians & Mathematicians", "innovation-and-technology", "cause-and-effect", "temporal-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Perseus's invention and systematic study of spiric sections expanded the conceptual toolkit of classical geometry, influencing later mathematical thought and demonstrating the advanced state of Hellenistic mathematical inquiry. His work, though partially lost, was recognized centuries later by Proclus, ensuring its place in the historical development of curve theory.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-14T18:40:06Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#29358031746", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Perseus was an ancient Greek geometer, who invented the concept of spiric sections, in analogy to the conic sections studied by Apollonius of Perga.", "newValue": "Perseus, an ancient Greek geometer active around 149–109 BCE, pioneered the systematic study of spiric sections, a class of curves generated by the intersection of a torus with a plane. His now-lost treatise, *On Spiric Lines* (Περὶ σπειρικῶν γραμμῶν), marked a significant expansion of classical Greek geometry beyond the conic sections explored by Apollonius of Perga. Operating within the rich intellectual tradition of Hellenistic mathematics, Perseus's innovations demonstrated a profound capacity for abstract spatial reasoning, solidifying his place as a notable, albeit often overlooked, figure in the history of mathematical thought.

Perseus's primary achievement was the rigorous analysis and classification of spiric sections, which include curves such as the hippopede (horse-fetter) and the Cassini oval, though he did not apply these specific names. He categorized these curves based on the relative position of the intersecting plane to the torus, paralleling Apollonius's method for conic sections. This sophisticated geometric exploration pushed the boundaries of what was considered solvable within Euclidean frameworks, showcasing the advanced state of mathematical inquiry in the Hellenistic period, likely centered in Alexandria or Athens.

While Perseus's original treatise has not survived, his contributions were acknowledged and referenced by later mathematicians, most notably Proclus in his commentary on Euclid's *Elements* around 450 CE. Proclus explicitly credits Perseus with the invention and study of spiric sections, thereby preserving knowledge of his work. This recognition underscores the lasting, if indirect, impact of Perseus's geometric innovations, which prefigured later developments in algebraic geometry and calculus, illustrating a continuous thread of inquiry into higher-order curves that eventually led to René Descartes' analytical geometry and the infinitesimal calculus of Newton and Leibniz."}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-14T18:40:06Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#29358031746", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "149 BCE, http://www.wikidata.org/.well-known/genid/64c373dfd1fb86b3776faa18249fec3b"}, {"title": "Accumulation of mathematical knowledge and formal systems", "type": "Idea", "year": "149 BCE, http://www.wikidata.org/.well-known/genid/64c373dfd1fb86b3776faa18249fec3b"}], "newValue": ["The established tradition of Greek geometry, particularly the study of conic sections by Apollonius of Perga, provided a foundational framework for Perseus's investigations.", "The intellectual environment of the Hellenistic period fostered advanced mathematical inquiry and encouraged the exploration of complex geometric problems.", "The existence of the torus as a fundamental geometric solid naturally prompted mathematicians to investigate its various planar intersections."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-14T18:40:06Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#29358031746", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Advanced mathematical knowledge used by subsequent scholars", "type": "Idea", "year": "109 BCE"}, {"title": "Contributed to